<p class="key">Green: target line · Orange: tractor · Blue: true working bar/path, passive ON · Grey dashed: implement, passive OFF · Cyan dot: measured working bar. Arrows show lateral force components, exaggerated to 12 m maximum: red draft, purple gravity, green soil resistance. Playback is recorded test data, not a browser guidance algorithm.</p>
<div class="tiles" id="tiles"></div><p id="readout"></p><canvas id="chart" width="1100" height="220" aria-label="True implement error with passive on and off"></canvas><p class="key">Error chart: blue ON, grey OFF, green applied tractor offset. Vertical cursor follows playback.</p><canvas id="forces" width="1100" height="200" aria-label="Lateral forces and side slope over time"></canvas><p class="key">Force chart: red asymmetric draft, purple gravitational component, green soil resistance (kN).</p><details><summary>Physical and sensor inputs for this seed</summary><pre id="settings"></pre></details></section>
<section><h2>All seeds and conditions</h2><p>Each condition runs with passive OFF and ON using the same seed. Evaluate truth, not noisy GPS, from t=30 s through the end, including terrain/soil transitions; entry-turn metrics use t=25–70 s to cover the turn. Mean bias distinguishes a persistent offset from weaving; weave SD is standard deviation, and line crossings require a 5 cm excursion on each side. Normal-case acceptance stays ≤20 cm straight or ≤30 cm curves and at least 30% RMS improvement. Stress cases expose limits; a bounded run does not mean acceptable tracking. Mass varies ±10% and lateral cornering stiffness ±20% between seeds. The playback uses seed 1; the table includes every seed.</p><div class="scroll"><table id="metrics"><thead><tr><th>Condition</th><th>Seed</th><th>Off RMS</th><th>On RMS</th><th>On P95</th><th>On peak</th><th>Mean bias</th><th>Weave SD</th><th>Cross/min</th><th>Offset at cap</th><th>Grip saturated</th><th>Engaged</th></tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div></section>
<section><h2>Model validity and guidance checks</h2><div class="scroll"><table id="checks"><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Check</th><th>Evidence</th></tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div></section>
<section><h2>What this represents</h2><p>Forces are in newtons, mass in kilograms, dimensions in metres. A constrained planar trailer has independent yaw inertia, a nonlinear slip-angle tire/soil force capped by available friction, downhill gravity, asymmetric draft at the working bar, and a bounded hitch-compliance approximation. Hitch reaction affects tractor slip and yaw. Production guidance runs at 10 Hz; physics runs at 100 Hz. GNSS defaults to 5 Hz with 150 ms implement delay, 100 ms tractor delay and 1.5 cm Gaussian position noise. Antenna height and roll shift are modelled; GNSS course is ground velocity, not tool body heading.</p>
<p><strong>Illustrative assumptions, not field calibration.</strong> The example implement is 8 m wide and 2,500 kg; these are not measurements of your implement. Soil coefficients, mass and draft force need real logs or measurements before numerical accuracy can predict your field. This is a reduced planar model, not full tyre mechanics, soil cutting, rutting, roll/pitch suspension, collision/rollover analysis or boundary/U-turn planning. Load-dependent speed change and hitch play are simplified proxies.</p><p>Force-balance background: <a href="https://www.mdpi.com/2624-7402/6/4/206">agricultural tractor–towed implement lateral dynamics</a> and <a href="https://eprints.lib.hokudai.ac.jp/repo/huscap/all/13144/68%281%29_p1-16.pdf">tractor–implement combinations on slopes</a>. This harness derives its own reduced equations and does not reuse their fitted coefficients or claim to reproduce their full models.</p><p>Raw CSV, all-seed metrics and editable draft-settings.json accompany the Windows harness.</p></section>
